  DailyReading(171, "Advice from a Caring Friend", "Proverbs 27:9", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "Perfume and incense bring joy to the heart; so does earnest counsel from a man’s friend."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "The proverb compares a friend's earnest counsel to the pleasure of perfume and incense. The counsel is valuable within relationship. Its warmth comes from sincere care, distinguishing it from advice used to control, belittle, or make the giver feel more important than the listener.\n\nNotice where the passage places its emphasis. God's character comes before the response it invites. Receiving these words is not a test of how spiritual you feel. You can approach them with questions, uncertainty, and a willingness to keep learning within the wider story of Scripture."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "Invite a trusted friend to tell you what they notice about a difficult choice. You can weigh their words thoughtfully without either accepting everything or dismissing the challenge.\n\nThere is room for an ordinary beginning. A sincere conversation, an offer of help, or a decision to pause before responding can express faith in everyday life. You do not need to solve the whole situation before doing the next loving and responsible thing."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can receive honest counsel from a friend who cares about your good, allowing their perspective to bring help even when it gently challenges what you hoped to hear.\n\nYou do not have to manufacture certainty to receive encouragement. Hold this truth alongside the parts of your life that remain unfinished. Hope can share space with grief, questions, and practical action; it need not silence any of them before it becomes meaningful."),
