  DailyReading(180, "A Mind Returning to Trust", "Isaiah 26:3", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "You will keep whoever’s mind is steadfast in perfect peace, because he trusts in you."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "The song connects steadfast trust with peace in God. It expresses the community's confidence in his keeping care. The verse invites dependence; it should not be used to blame people with anxiety or suggest that prayer replaces counseling, treatment, or other appropriate support.\n\nNotice where the passage places its emphasis. God's character comes before the response it invites. Receiving these words is not a test of how spiritual you feel. You can approach them with questions, uncertainty, and a willingness to keep learning within the wider story of Scripture."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "A brief prayer can accompany grounding practices and trusted care. When your attention wanders back to worry, return gently rather than adding self-criticism to the distress.\n\nThere is room for an ordinary beginning. A sincere conversation, an offer of help, or a decision to pause before responding can express faith in everyday life. You do not need to solve the whole situation before doing the next loving and responsible thing."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can practice returning your attention toward God with patience, without interpreting persistent anxiety as a measure of your worth, effort, or the sincerity of your faith.\n\nLet this be a steady reminder rather than another demand to perform well. Remembering God's care can make space for a more patient response to yourself and to others. Today's limits are real, and there can still be something faithful and good within them."),
    ReadingCard("prayer",
        "God of peace, you know the concerns I carry. Help me receive your care without pretending that everything is resolved. Give me patience in uncertainty, clarity for what is mine to do, and the grace to pause before I react. Amen.\n\nCarry this with you: You can practice returning your attention toward God with patience, without interpreting persistent anxiety as a measure of your worth, effort, or the sincerity of your faith."),
  ]),
