        "Acts describes a community forming around repeated acts of learning and belonging. These practices gave faith a shared shape. You do not need a dramatic event every day; steady attention and connection can help sustain your life with God.\n\nRead this passage as part of the larger biblical story. Its comfort is not a promise that life will become easy or that faithful people will avoid loss. It points toward a relationship with God that can sustain honest trust within real circumstances."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "Choose one shared practice to make room for this week. A conversation, a meal, or a prayer with someone else can support your ordinary faith.\n\nYou may find that this becomes clearer through practice rather than through a sudden feeling. Let one interaction today be shaped by patience and attention. If the day is difficult, begin again gently; a hard moment does not erase the possibility of a faithful next step."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can grow through simple practices shared with other believers. The early church continued in teaching, fellowship, meals, and prayer rather than relying only on private inspiration.\n\nThis truth can travel with you beyond these few minutes. When attention scatters or the day changes unexpectedly, return to what the passage actually says. You are allowed to grow slowly, to seek wise counsel, and to hold hope without pretending to know the outcome."),
    ReadingCard("prayer",
        "Loving God, help me receive and extend care with humility. Teach me to listen well, honor healthy boundaries, and recognize the needs of people near me. Give me courage to ask for support and generosity to offer the help I can. Amen.\n\nCarry this with you: You can grow through simple practices shared with other believers. The early church continued in teaching, fellowship, meals, and prayer rather than relying only on private inspiration."),
  ]),
  DailyReading(251, "What You Have Can Serve We", "Acts 4:32", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "The multitude of those who believed were of one heart and soul. Not one of them claimed that anything of the things which he possessed was his own, but they had all things in common."),
