  DailyReading(252, "Comfort Can Help You Grow", "Acts 9:31", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "So the assemblies throughout all Judea, Galilee, and Samaria had peace and were built up. They were multiplied, walking in the fear of the Lord and in the comfort of the Holy Spirit."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "Strengthening did not come only through pressure or difficulty. The churches also experienced peace and the Spirit's comfort. This picture makes room for growth that includes receiving care, rather than demanding that you always become stronger through strain.\n\nNotice where the passage places its emphasis. God's character comes before the response it invites. Receiving these words is not a test of how spiritual you feel. You can approach them with questions, uncertainty, and a willingness to keep learning within the wider story of Scripture."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "Notice one form of healthy encouragement available today. You can accept it as support for faith instead of assuming you ought to manage without any help.\n\nThere is room for an ordinary beginning. A sincere conversation, an offer of help, or a decision to pause before responding can express faith in everyday life. You do not need to solve the whole situation before doing the next loving and responsible thing."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can welcome comfort as part of a faithful life. Acts describes the church growing through reverence for God and the encouragement of the Holy Spirit.\n\nYou do not have to manufacture certainty to receive encouragement. Hold this truth alongside the parts of your life that remain unfinished. Hope can share space with grief, questions, and practical action; it need not silence any of them before it becomes meaningful."),
