  ]),
  DailyReading(270, "Grace Meets an Unremoved Limit", "2 Corinthians 12:9", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "He has said to me, “My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.” Most gladly therefore I will rather glory in my weaknesses, that the power of Christ may rest on me."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "Paul had asked for a burden to be removed, yet the answer directed him toward grace within it. This does not forbid seeking treatment or change. It means God's presence need not wait until every persistent difficulty has been resolved.\n\nNotice where the passage places its emphasis. God's character comes before the response it invites. Receiving these words is not a test of how spiritual you feel. You can approach them with questions, uncertainty, and a willingness to keep learning within the wider story of Scripture."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "You can ask for relief and receive support in the present at the same time. Let one practical accommodation make today's responsibility more manageable for you.\n\nThere is room for an ordinary beginning. A sincere conversation, an offer of help, or a decision to pause before responding can express faith in everyday life. You do not need to solve the whole situation before doing the next loving and responsible thing."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
