    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "John's desire is that believers avoid sin, yet he makes room for the reality of failure. Christ's help does not make harm unimportant; it keeps repentance from becoming despair. You can return honestly and ask how to respond differently with support.\n\nRead this passage as part of the larger biblical story. Its comfort is not a promise that life will become easy or that faithful people will avoid loss. It points toward a relationship with God that can sustain honest trust within real circumstances."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "After a mistake today, choose confession over hiding and repair over self-punishment. You can ask for help with a recurring pattern before it becomes more entrenched.\n\nYou may find that this becomes clearer through practice rather than through a sudden feeling. Let one interaction today be shaped by patience and attention. If the day is difficult, begin again gently; a hard moment does not erase the possibility of a faithful next step."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can take wrongdoing seriously without deciding that one failure ends your relationship with God. John points toward Jesus as the Counselor with the Father.\n\nLet this be a steady reminder rather than another demand to perform well. Remembering God's care can make space for a more patient response to yourself and to others. Today's limits are real, and there can still be something faithful and good within them."),
    ReadingCard("prayer",
        "Merciful God, help me receive grace without hiding what needs to change. Give me courage to be honest, patience with the work of growth, and compassion for others who are learning too. Let your kindness shape the way I begin again today. Amen.\n\nCarry this with you: You can take wrongdoing seriously without deciding that one failure ends your relationship with God. John points toward Jesus as the Counselor with the Father."),
  ]),
  DailyReading(350, "Look Again at the Love Given", "1 John 3:1", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God! For this cause the world doesn’t know us, because it didn’t know him."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "The invitation begins with seeing how great the Father's love is. Belonging is presented as something given, not something established by public recognition. Even when others misunderstand your faith or overlook your worth, their judgment does not create or cancel the Father's love.\n\nThe setting matters: Scripture speaks into human lives, communities, questions, and struggles. Let the words keep their original meaning before making a connection to your own day. Faithfulness grows through patient attention, rather than through forcing a quick answer from a single verse."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "Pause before measuring yourself by today's approval. You can carry the responsibilities in front of you as someone loved, rather than as an audition for belonging.\n\nYou may find that this becomes clearer through practice rather than through a sudden feeling. Let one interaction today be shaped by patience and attention. If the day is difficult, begin again gently; a hard moment does not erase the possibility of a faithful next step."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can receive the name child of God as a gift of love, rather than a title earned by being impressive. John asks you to notice its wonder.\n\nLet this be a steady reminder rather than another demand to perform well. Remembering God's care can make space for a more patient response to yourself and to others. Today's limits are real, and there can still be something faithful and good within them."),
    ReadingCard("prayer",
        "Faithful God, help me attend to your words with an open heart. Give me wisdom to understand, courage to live with integrity, and kindness toward the people I meet. Hold my questions as I learn to trust you through the ordinary decisions of today. Amen.\n\nCarry this with you: You can receive the name child of God as a gift of love, rather than a title earned by being impressive. John asks you to notice its wonder."),
  ]),
  DailyReading(351, "Make Love True in Practice", "1 John 3:18", [
    ReadingCard("scripture",
        "My little children, let’s not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth."),
    ReadingCard("meaning",
        "Words matter, but they can become a substitute for responding to need. Love in truth asks whether your promise matches what you will do. You can offer something modest and reliable rather than generous language that leaves another person waiting without support.\n\nNotice where the passage places its emphasis. God's character comes before the response it invites. Receiving these words is not a test of how spiritual you feel. You can approach them with questions, uncertainty, and a willingness to keep learning within the wider story of Scripture."),
    ReadingCard("encouragement",
        "Choose one caring promise you can keep today. If you cannot do what you offered, communicate honestly and help find a workable alternative instead of quietly disappearing.\n\nThere is room for an ordinary beginning. A sincere conversation, an offer of help, or a decision to pause before responding can express faith in everyday life. You do not need to solve the whole situation before doing the next loving and responsible thing."),
    ReadingCard("hope",
        "You can make love more credible through a concrete response. John joins love with deed and truth, inviting care that a person can actually experience.\n\nThis truth can travel with you beyond these few minutes. When attention scatters or the day changes unexpectedly, return to what the passage actually says. You are allowed to grow slowly, to seek wise counsel, and to hold hope without pretending to know the outcome."),
    ReadingCard("prayer",
